---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { socials } from '../data/socials';

// Bios published 2026-07-25 from her existing drafts (short = speaker-bio.md
// verbatim; long = speaker-bio-devoxx-morocco.md with the team-bootstrapping
// clause removed — it was word-for-word her LinkedIn line, and she avoids
// verbatim reuse across public surfaces).
// Headshot: /headshot.jpg (800x800, added 2026-07-24; same source as the OG card).

const email = socials.find((s) => s.icon === 'email');
---

<Layout title="Speaker kit" description="Bio, headshot and facts for event organizers." withBack>
  <section class="kit">
    <h1 class="title title--page" data-animate>Speaker kit</h1>

    <div data-animate>
      <h2 class="section-title">Short bio</h2>
      <p class="kit-text">
        Simona Oancea has spent over a decade building backend systems where failure
        is expensive. Gaming platforms. Financial infrastructure. The kind of software
        where architecture isn't an academic exercise, it's the thing that wakes you
        up at night.
      </p>
      <p class="kit-text">
        Most teams treat architecture as something you get right at the start and then
        defend. She thinks the opposite: that the mark of a well-built system is how
        gracefully it lets you change your mind.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div data-animate>
      <h2 class="section-title">Long bio</h2>
      <p class="kit-text">
        Simona Oancea has spent a decade building backend systems in the parts of the
        industry where being wrong is expensive — banking, payments, and large-scale
        gaming. She's now at Deutsche Bank, leading the migration of a deal management
        system in investment banking.
      </p>
      <p class="kit-text">
        The lesson she's drawn from all of them is this: it isn't the size of an
        architecture decision that hurts you. It's how reversible it is. The famous
        ones — the framework debates, the database choices — usually get the scrutiny.
        What does the damage is the choice you made on a Tuesday afternoon and didn't
        realize was architecture until three years later, when it was already
        load-bearing.
      </p>
      <p class="kit-text">
        Most teams get this wrong because they treat architecture as something you get
        right at the start and then defend. She thinks the opposite — the mark of a
        well-built system is how gracefully it lets you change your mind. The
        decisions that matter most are the ones you can undo.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div data-animate>
      <h2 class="section-title">Headshot</h2>
      <img class="kit-headshot" src="/headshot.jpg" alt="Simona Oancea" width="400" height="400" />
      <p class="kit-text">
        <a href="/headshot.jpg" download="simona-oancea.jpg">Download the full-size headshot</a>
        (800×800 JPEG).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div data-animate>
      <h2 class="section-title">Facts</h2>
      <ul class="work-list">
        <li class="work-item">
          <span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span class="work-text">Based in <strong>Bucharest, Romania</strong></span>
        </li>
        <li class="work-item">
          <span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span class="work-text">Speaks <strong>Romanian and English</strong></span>
        </li>
        <li class="work-item">
          <span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span class="work-text">
            Talks about <strong>software architecture, large-scale distributed systems,
            backend engineering, enterprise Java, and agentic engineering</strong>
          </span>
        </li>
        {
          email && (
            <li class="work-item">
              <span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              <span class="work-text">
                Booking: <a class="inline-link" href={email.url}>email</a>
              </span>
            </li>
          )
        }
        <li class="work-item">
          <span class="bullet" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span class="work-text">
            Speaker profile on{' '}
            <a class="inline-link" href="https://sessionize.com/simona/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              Sessionize
            </a>
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
